 ],
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "In Santos, Brazil, the beachfront looks like a row of dominoes about to fall.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Around sixty five apartment towers there are visibly leaning.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And no, it's not the sea wind. It's what's under them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Most were built in the fifties and sixties, on short concrete footings.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The top few meters are firm sand. Great, the builders thought.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But under the sand is a thick layer of soft sea clay, like wet toothpaste.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The weight of a tower spreads down into that clay, and slowly squeezes the water out.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the building sinks, a few millimeters a year, for decades.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now put two towers side by side. Under the gap, their weights overlap.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The clay between them gets squeezed twice, so each tower sinks more on that side.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why so many lean toward each other, like tired friends.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They're watched closely, and most aren't about to fall.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "One of them, the Nuncio Malzoni, was pushed back upright with giant hydraulic jacks in 2001.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Today, new towers there sit on piles that go all the way through the clay.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Down to the hard ground below, where nothing squeezes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
